course: dictionary
lesson: 14954
title: Relatively: compared with what?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Relatively» means compared with something else. It tells you that a size, speed or amount is judged against another one, so it is not the full picture on its own.
explain: «Our school is relatively small.» Small compared with what? Probably other schools, or the big school in the next town. The word asks you to think about a comparison, even if the sentence does not say it.
explain: «Relatively speaking, the walk is short.» This phrase means compared with other things. It is a helpful clue that the writer is comparing, not stating an exact amount.
explain: To check your idea, swap in «fairly» or «quite». If the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning. Then ask yourself: compared with what?
fact: Relatively means quite or fairly, or compared with other things.
fact: «Relatively» is an adverb, so it tells us more about an adjective like «small» or a verb like «moves».
fact: «A relatively small school» means a school that is not very small, but small when you compare it with other schools.
fact: «Relatively speaking» is a phrase that means compared with other things.
fact: A good check is to ask «compared with what?» If you can answer that question, «relatively» fits.
exercise: «The test was relatively easy for the class.» What does relatively mean here? | *Fairly easy, compared with other tests | Very, very hard | Written in pencil | Done in a loud voice
exercise: «Relatively speaking, the mountain is short.» What does relatively speaking mean? | *Compared with other things | Speaking all at once | Speaking in a foreign language | Speaking very quietly
exercise: Which word can replace relatively in «a relatively small school»? | never | *fairly | slowly | under
test: «Tom's bag is relatively light.» What does this tell us? | *It is fairly light, compared with other bags | It is the lightest bag in the world | It is light as a feather in every way | It is completely empty
test: «Dad's car is relatively new.» Which swap keeps the meaning best? | never new | very dark | slowly new | *fairly new
exam: Which question helps you understand the word relatively? | *Compared with what? | How loud is it? | Who wrote it? | Where is it kept?
